---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../../components/Callout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Password Auth">
  <h1>Password Authentication</h1>
  <p>Email + password is the default first-factor flow. Identity hashes passwords with bcrypt cost 12, enforces strength rules on signup, and locks accounts after repeated failed logins.</p>

  <h2>Signup</h2>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/PasswordSignup \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "email": "alice@example.com",
    "password": "Sup3rStrong!Pass",
    "recovery_email": "alice-backup@example.com"
  }'</code></pre>
  <p>Response (<code>PasswordSignupResponse</code>):</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-json">{
  "user": {
    "id": "user_01HX...",
    "email": "alice@example.com",
    "name": "alice",
    "role": "member",
    "status": "USER_STATUS_ACTIVE"
  },
  "accessToken": "eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs...",
  "refreshToken": "rt_...",
  "expiresIn": 900
}</code></pre>

  <Callout type="info" title="When local auth is disabled">
    With <code>GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL=false</code>, both <code>PasswordSignup</code> and <code>PasswordLogin</code> return <code>ErrLocalAuthDisabled</code>. Use this in deployments where only OAuth or passkey logins are allowed.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Login</h2>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/PasswordLogin \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"Sup3rStrong!Pass"}'</code></pre>
  <p>Response shape branches on whether the user has TOTP enabled:</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-json">// Without 2FA
{
  "user": { ... },
  "accessToken": "eyJ...",
  "refreshToken": "rt_...",
  "expiresIn": 900,
  "totpRequired": false
}

// With 2FA enabled — no tokens; must call VerifyTotp next
{
  "user": { ... },
  "totpRequired": true,
  "loginChallengeId": "lc_..."
}</code></pre>

  <h2>Password requirements</h2>
  <p>Enforced by <code>pkg/passwords.ValidateStrength</code>. A password must:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Be at least 8 characters and at most 128 characters.</li>
    <li>Contain at least one uppercase letter.</li>
    <li>Contain at least one lowercase letter.</li>
    <li>Contain at least one digit.</li>
    <li>Contain at least one special character from <code>!@#$%^&amp;*()_+-=[]&#123;&#125;;':"\\|,.&lt;&gt;/?`~</code>.</li>
    <li>Not appear in the built-in top-100 common-passwords blocklist.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Failures surface as <code>ErrInvalidArgument</code> with one or more issue strings.</p>

  <h2>Hashing</h2>
  <p>Passwords are hashed with bcrypt at cost factor 12 (~250&nbsp;ms on modern hardware). Stored as standard bcrypt strings (<code>$2a$12$...</code>) on the <code>User</code> node. Plaintext passwords never touch logs.</p>

  <h2>Failed-login lockout</h2>
  <p>Each failed <code>PasswordLogin</code> increments a counter on the User node. After <code>GATEWAY_LOGIN_MAX_FAILED_ATTEMPTS</code> (default 5) failures, the account is locked for <code>GATEWAY_LOGIN_LOCKOUT_SECONDS</code> (default 900). Successful login resets the counter to zero.</p>

  <h2>Change password</h2>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/ChangePassword \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ACCESS" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "current_password": "Sup3rStrong!Pass",
    "new_password": "Even5tronger#Now"
  }'</code></pre>
  <p>The current password is verified before the new one is accepted. Audit event: <code>password_changed</code>.</p>

  <h2>Password reset</h2>
  <p>The self-serve flow is recovery-email driven and never enumerates accounts:</p>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/RequestPasswordReset \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com"}'
# Always 200 OK, regardless of whether the user exists or has a recovery email.</code></pre>
  <p>If the user exists and has a <code>recovery_email</code> on file, a reset link is sent via the email service. Token validity: <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORD_RESET_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> (default 3600).</p>

  <h2>Error catalog</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Domain error</th><th>Connect code</th><th>Trigger</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ErrInvalidArgument</code></td><td>invalid_argument</td><td>Missing email/password, malformed email, weak password.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ErrAlreadyExists</code></td><td>already_exists</td><td>Signup with an email that's already registered.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ErrUnauthenticated</code></td><td>unauthenticated</td><td>Wrong password, no password set on user, missing/expired token on auth'd RPCs.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ErrAccountLocked</code></td><td>permission_denied</td><td>Too many failed logins; lockout still active.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ErrAccountDeactivated</code></td><td>permission_denied</td><td>User <code>status=deactivated</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ErrLocalAuthDisabled</code></td><td>permission_denied</td><td><code>GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL=false</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ErrInvalidTotpCode</code></td><td>unauthenticated</td><td>Login challenge present but the TOTP code is wrong / expired.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Example scenarios</h2>

  <h3>1. New user signup &rarr; first login</h3>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash"># Signup — gets tokens immediately
curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/PasswordSignup \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"Sup3rStrong!Pass"}'
# &rarr; { user, accessToken, refreshToken, expiresIn: 900 }

# (15 minutes later, access token expires)
# Refresh the access token
curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/RefreshToken \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"refresh_token":"rt_..."}'
# &rarr; { user, accessToken, refreshToken (rotated) }</code></pre>

  <h3>2. Existing user login</h3>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/PasswordLogin \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"Sup3rStrong!Pass"}'
# &rarr; tokens (no totp_required since 2FA is off for this user)</code></pre>

  <h3>3. Account-locked retry</h3>
  <pre is:raw><code class="language-bash"># Five wrong passwords in a row...
for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do
  curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/PasswordLogin \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"WrongOne!"}'
done
# Sixth attempt — even with the right password — fails until lockout expires:
curl -s -X POST http://localhost/identity.IdentityService/PasswordLogin \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email":"alice@example.com","password":"Sup3rStrong!Pass"}'
# &rarr; permission_denied: account locked, retry after 900s</code></pre>
  <p>The lockout window can be cut short by an admin using <code>ResetUserPassword</code> (which clears the failed-login counter as a side effect).</p>
</DocsLayout>
